{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1164 Raymond was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and spent nearly ten years in captivity. He was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nUpon his return Raymond became regent for Bohemond III of Antioch during Bohemond’s minority. He also served as regent for the County of Tripoli when Raymond II’s heir Bohemond was still a child. Raymond III played a key role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was involved in conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim powers. In 1180 he married Echive of Armenia the daughter of Thoros II of Armenia. This alliance strengthened ties between Tripoli and Armenia.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III is best known for his role in the lead-up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He invited Saladin to intervene in a dispute with Guy of Lusignan over the Kingdom of Jerusalem. This invitation led to Saladin’s advance and the eventual Muslim victory at Hattin. Following the disaster Raymond barely escaped with his life. He died in 1187 without a male heir leaving Tripoli to his cousin Raymond IV of Toulouse.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III’s reign was marked by instability and conflict. Despite his efforts to secure the Crusader states his decisions contributed to their decline. His death marked the end of the original County of Tripoli’s independence as it soon fell to Muslim forces.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.1741926521062851}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -0.8525660634040833}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 